# November 28, 2026: Cheyenne, Wyoming. The buildings named in one walk-through video of the town's old buildings
# (the record is source/videos/20230526_NsindAXmRv4.md), each checked in sources opened for this batch; the video
# itself is a tip and nothing in these entries comes from it.
# Sources: National Register records and forms (Downtown Cheyenne Historic District and its 1996 boundary increase,
# Fort D. A. Russell and its National Historic Landmark form, the Masonic Temple, the City and County Building);
# Wikipedia; SAH Archipedia (Buildings of Wyoming); Cinema Treasures; Cowboy State Daily; the Wyoming State Parks page
# for Curt Gowdy State Park; the Laramie County Library research guide; the owners' own pages where they would open.
# Pins: ArcGIS geocoder points for street addresses, Wikipedia and OpenStreetMap points, each looked at on the aerial.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'cheyenne_sgfb','label':'Cheyenne, Wyoming','added':'2026-11-28 06:49'}
def NR(n,t): return ['National Register record: '+t,'https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/'+n]
DT=NR('96000909','Downtown Cheyenne Historic District (boundary increase, 1996)')
DTW=W('Downtown Cheyenne Historic District')
DOWNTOWN='Downtown Cheyenne, inside the Downtown Cheyenne Historic District. Street parking and public lots round the blocks.'
PLACES=[
N('plains-hotel-cheyenne','The Plains Hotel','Cheyenne','wy','hotel','standing',41.13324,-104.81473,'exact','open','walk','hotels','1910–',
  "A five-story hotel at Sixteenth Street (Lincolnway) and Central Avenue, begun in June 1910 and opened in March 1911 to designs by the Cheyenne architect William Dubois. The 1996 Register form describes blonde brick walls with terra-cotta belt courses and a cut-stone cornice; a five-story annex of 1927 to 1928 stands beside it on Sixteenth Street. It is part of the Downtown Cheyenne Historic District and is still a working hotel, restored in 2002 and 2003 by the accounts found.",
  'Blonde brick with terra-cotta bands and a stone cornice, and the 1920s annex next door. The lobby of a working hotel; how much of the original interior survives was not checked.',
  '','1600 Central Avenue. A working hotel: the public rooms are those of any hotel. '+DOWNTOWN,
  [W('The Plains Hotel'),DT,DTW],brick='no',bcol='buff',arch='William Dubois',archw='1996 Register form: "designed by Cheyenne architect William DuBois"',hours='?'),
N('tivoli-building-cheyenne','Tivoli Building','Cheyenne','wy','civic','standing',41.13222,-104.81639,'exact','view','walk','','1892–',
  "A three-story brick and stone saloon building of 1892 on the corner of Sixteenth Street (Lincolnway) and Carey Avenue, with an oriel window and an eight-sided turret, its roofs covered in pressed metal. It held a saloon (a speakeasy in Prohibition), and the committee that planned the first Cheyenne Frontier Days in 1897 met upstairs. Vacant for a time after the 1960s, it was restored by the Cheyenne Chamber of Commerce, finished in 1981; the 1996 Register form calls it the crown jewel of the downtown district. As of 2012 a brewpub was on the ground floor; what it holds now was not checked.",
  'The corner turret, the oriel and the carved stone. Interior not checked.',
  '','301 West Lincolnway (West Sixteenth Street), at Carey Avenue. Seen from the street; whether a business inside is open to visitors was not checked. '+DOWNTOWN,
  [W('Tivoli Building (Cheyenne, Wyoming)'),DT,DTW],brick='?'),
N('hynds-building-cheyenne','Hynds Building','Cheyenne','wy','civic','standing',41.13291,-104.81571,'exact','view','walk','','1919–',
  "A five-story steel-framed office block of 1919 to 1922 by William Dubois, built for the businessman Harry P. Hynds on the corner of Capitol Avenue and Sixteenth Street, facing the Union Pacific depot. It stands where the Inter-Ocean Hotel burned in 1916, and Hynds wanted it fireproof. Its two street fronts are faced in terra-cotta tile, with his name over the arched entrance. Offices left by the late 1980s, and it has stood empty since; in February 2026 Cowboy State Daily reported a plan to turn it and the burned-out lot beside it into housing.",
  'Terra-cotta fronts with a crowning cornice, a Tudor-arched entrance with "Harry P. Hynds" above it, a steel canopy over the shopfronts with a stained-glass transom. Empty; no visitors.',
  '','1602 Capitol Avenue, at West Lincolnway, across from the depot. Private and empty: seen from the street only. '+DOWNTOWN,
  [['SAH Archipedia: Hynds Building','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/23037'],['Cowboy State Daily: a solution for the Hynds and "the Hole" (February 2026)','https://cowboystatedaily.com/2026/02/12/finally-a-solution-for-cheyennes-infamous-hynds-hole-workforce-housing/'],DT],
  brick='?',arch='William Dubois',archw='SAH Archipedia: "1919–1922, William R. Dubois"'),
N('paramount-capitol-avenue-theatre-cheyenne','Capitol Avenue Theatre (the Paramount Building)','Cheyenne','wy','civic','standing',41.13311,-104.81533,'exact','open','walk','','1903–',
  "A four-story pressed-brick theatre building by William Dubois, built for the Capitol Avenue Theater Company, whose stockholders included F. E. Warren and J. M. Carey; the 1996 Register form dates it to 1905 and Cinema Treasures says it opened that year (a local radio station gives 1904). Fire ruined the inside in 1915, and in 1930 it was remade into a movie house and renamed the Paramount. Fire destroyed the auditorium in 1980; the front part survives with the Paramount marquee over the door and a café at street level.",
  'The front block with its marquee. The auditorium is gone.',
  '','1607 Capitol Avenue. The café on the ground floor is open to customers; hours from the café\'s own page, which may be out of date. '+DOWNTOWN,
  [['Cinema Treasures: Paramount Theatre','https://cinematreasures.org/theaters/20610'],DT,['The Paramount Café','https://wolverine-bird-d6k4.squarespace.com']],
  brick='?',arch='William Dubois',archw='1996 Register form: "William Dubois was the architect"',hours='Café: Monday to Saturday 7:30 a.m. to 6 p.m.'),
N('knights-of-pythias-building-cheyenne','Knights of Pythias Building','Cheyenne','wy','civic','standing',41.13311,-104.81768,'site','view','walk','','1884–',
  "A lodge building of 1884 to 1885 on West Seventeenth Street. The 1996 Register form says the eastern two-thirds had been covered with modern metal, while the western part kept its original front with a heavy metal cornice and carved window heads. The ground floor has held a pharmacy, a furniture store and a flea market; in 2024 KOWB reported that the Knights still use the upper floors and the basement.",
  'The western bay keeps the 1880s front, cornice and carved window caps. Interior not checked.',
  '','312 West 17th Street. Private: seen from the street. '+DOWNTOWN,
  [DT,['KOWB: Hauntings of Cheyenne, the Knights of Pythias (2024)','https://kowb1290.com/ixp/98/p/hauntings-cheyenne-knights-of-pythias/']],brick='?'),
N('masonic-temple-cheyenne-wy','Masonic Temple','Cheyenne','wy','civic','standing',41.13517,-104.81712,'exact','view','walk','','1901–',
  "A three-story Second Renaissance Revival lodge of 1901 at Nineteenth Street and Capitol Avenue, of buff brick veneer on a concrete foundation. It burned in 1903 and was rebuilt with few changes, and a matching addition with a stage went on the west side in 1929. The Register form of 1984 says the Masons had kept its character, and notes that the old buildings round it had given way to parking lots.",
  'Buff brick, a raised brick arch over the main door and a stone panel inscribed "Masonic Temple". Interior not checked.',
  '','1820 Capitol Avenue. A working lodge: seen from the street.',
  [W('Masonic Temple (Cheyenne, Wyoming)'),NR('84000162','Masonic Temple'),DTW],brick='no',bcol='buff'),
N('city-county-building-cheyenne','City and County Building','Cheyenne','wy','civic','standing',41.13505,-104.81880,'exact','view','walk','','1917–',
  "Built in 1917 to 1919 to hold the governments of Laramie County and the City of Cheyenne under one roof, with the district court, to designs by William Dubois; John W. Howard is named with him in the Register record. It is Classical Revival, with a rusticated limestone base, brick above, pediments over the doors and twelve Ionic columns. The Register form says stone came from Bedford, Indiana and marble from Salt Lake City.",
  'The columned south and east fronts on a limestone base. Interior not checked.',
  '','19th Street and Carey Avenue. A government building still in use; its hours were not checked.',
  [W('City and County Building (Cheyenne, Wyoming)'),NR('78002828','City and County Building')],
  brick='?',arch='William Dubois',builder='John W. Howard',archw='National Register record: Dubois, William; Howard, John W.'),
N('st-marys-cathedral-cheyenne','Cathedral of St. Mary','Cheyenne','wy','worship','standing',41.13773,-104.81776,'exact','open','walk','','1906–',
  "The Catholic cathedral of Wyoming, begun in 1906 and consecrated on January 31, 1909, to designs by Fisher and Lowery of Omaha; Moses Patrick Keefe built it of sandstone quarried at Iron Mountain, north of the city. It is Gothic Revival, 135 by 70 feet, with side galleries and a rear organ loft, and a ceiling of steel and concrete arches faced with oak. The parish began in 1867 in a frame church.",
  'Sandstone walls; inside, stained-glass windows made in Europe (the Madonna window in the organ loft was given by the Knights of Columbus) and a 1991 organ by Visser-Rowland.',
  '','2107 Capitol Avenue. Open for Mass; the times were read as a web search quoted the cathedral\'s own Mass Times page, which would not open directly.',
  [W("St. Mary's Catholic Cathedral (Cheyenne, Wyoming)"),['Cathedral of St. Mary: Mass times','https://stmarycathedral.com/mass-times']],
  brick='no',arch='Fisher and Lowery',builder='Moses Patrick Keefe',archw='Wikipedia: the architectural firm of Fisher and Lowery from Omaha; Keefe was responsible for constructing it',
  hours='Mass: Sat 4 and 6 p.m.; Sun 8 a.m., 10 a.m., noon, 5 p.m.; Mon–Fri 6:45 a.m. and 5:15 p.m.'),
N('fort-da-russell-warren-afb','Fort D. A. Russell (F. E. Warren Air Force Base)','Cheyenne','wy','fort','standing',41.15455,-104.86275,'area','permit','drive','forts','1867–',
  "Set up in 1867 to guard the building of the Union Pacific, made permanent in 1884, and one of the largest cavalry posts in the country by 1900; the Buffalo Soldiers of the 9th and 10th Cavalry and the 24th Infantry served here. Renamed Fort Francis E. Warren in 1930, it has been F. E. Warren Air Force Base since 1949. The National Historic Landmark form of 1975 says nothing of the first post is left, but most of the red brick quarters, offices and stables of 1885 and later survive round six parade grounds. Among them is the brick cavalry drill hall of 1907, an indoor riding arena later made a commissary and then a gymnasium.",
  'Rows of red brick quarters, barracks and stables of 1885 to about 1913 round the parade grounds, and the 1907 drill hall with its monitor roof and round-arched gables.',
  '','An active Air Force base: there is no public entry. Visitors without base access go only as guests of the base museum (see the post hospital museum), arranged ahead. Never try to enter without a pass. The pin marks the historic district only roughly.',
  [W('Fort D. A. Russell (Wyoming)'),NR('69000191','Fort David A. Russell'),['National Historic Landmark form (1975)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000191_text'],['SAH Archipedia: Freedom Hall (the drill hall)','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/23032']],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',apart='fort-russell-post-hospital-museum: the museum is one building of the fort, given its own entry because it is the part visitors can enter'),
N('fort-russell-post-hospital-museum','Fort D. A. Russell post hospital (Warren ICBM and Heritage Museum)','Cheyenne','wy','fort','standing',41.15630,-104.86430,'site','open','drive','forts','1887–',
  "Building 31 was built in 1887 as the post hospital, shaped like a T, and given wings in 1905 to make the E it is now; the fort was one of three posts that trained the Army's new hospital stewards from 1889. It became eleven apartments in 1928, was emptied about 1960 and stripped for parts, and was restored from 2006 to 2013 to near its first look for the base museum.",
  'A brick building on a raised limestone base with hipped roofs, segmental brick arches over the windows and a wooden veranda; in the kitchen wing, the original cooking fireplace.',
  '','401 Champagne Drive, Building 31, inside F. E. Warren Air Force Base. Free. Visitors without base access must contact the museum at least 72 business hours ahead with their full name, birth date and driver\'s licence number (a REAL ID is needed). Hours and rules as a web search quoted the museum\'s own visiting page, which would not open directly; the museum says its hours can change.',
  [['SAH Archipedia: Warren ICBM and Heritage Museum','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/23030'],['Warren ICBM and Heritage Museum: how to visit','https://www.warrenmuseum.com/contactinfo/'],['National Historic Landmark form (1975)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000191_text']],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',hours='Monday to Friday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed weekends and federal holidays',
  apart='fort-da-russell-warren-afb: one building of the fort, the one visitors can enter'),
N('cheyenne-va-hospital-district','Cheyenne Veterans Administration Hospital','Cheyenne','wy','inst','standing',41.14828,-104.78550,'site','view','drive','','1932–',
  "A 50-acre veterans' hospital campus on Pershing Boulevard whose main building of 1932, at the centre and facing south, is in the Mission and Spanish Colonial Revival styles: tapestry brick in five-course American bond under Spanish tile roofs. Fifteen buildings, a structure and an object were listed together on the National Register in 2013, as part of a nationwide study of veterans' hospitals.",
  'The tile-roofed 1932 main building and the older buildings round it. Interior not checked.',
  '','2360 East Pershing Boulevard. A working medical centre: seen from the public way; it is not a place to wander for sightseeing.',
  [W('Cheyenne Veterans Administration Hospital Historic District'),NR('13000239','Cheyenne Veterans Administration Hospital Historic District')],brick='?'),
N('granite-springs-dam-wy','Granite Springs Dam','Curt Gowdy State Park','wy','water','standing',41.17461,-105.22381,'exact','public','drive','','1904–',
  "The masonry dam that holds Granite Springs Reservoir on Middle Crow Creek, part of Cheyenne's water supply from the Laramie Mountains. The national dam inventory, as Snoflo gives it, says it was finished in 1904 and stands 90 feet high; its date was not checked in any other source. The reservoir and its neighbour Crystal Reservoir became Granite State Park in 1971, through a lease with the City of Cheyenne, renamed Curt Gowdy State Park in 1972.",
  'The stone face of the dam below the reservoir, capped with later concrete. How close a visitor can get to the face was not checked.',
  '','Curt Gowdy State Park, run by Wyoming State Parks, off Happy Jack Road (Highway 210) about 24 miles west of Cheyenne. A state park fee is paid at the fee booths or self-pay kiosks. A working city water dam: keep off the structure.',
  [W('Curt Gowdy State Park'),['Wyoming State Parks: about Curt Gowdy','https://wyoparks.wyo.gov/index.php/about-curt-gowdy'],['Snoflo: Granite Springs Dam','https://snoflo.org/reservoir/wyoming/granite-springs']],
  brick='?',conf='mixed',hours='Park open all year, day and night (camping needs a permit); visitor center Wed–Sun 9 a.m.–4:30 p.m. in the cold season'),
N('idelman-mansion-cheyenne','Idelman Mansion','Cheyenne','wy','landmark','standing',41.13925,-104.82056,'exact','view','walk','','1892–',
  "A house on Carey Avenue built between 1892 and 1894 (the Sanborn maps of those years show the lot empty and then built on) for Max Idelman, president of Idelman Brothers, the liquor dealers, on two lots across from the new Capitol that he bought from the Carey family in 1887. It was later Schrader's Funeral Home. Carey Avenue was once called Millionaires Row, and a 2023 newsletter calls this the only house of that row still standing. A Laramie County Library research guide says it is used as the Governor's Office.",
  'The house and its later addition, from the street. Interior not checked.',
  '','2323 Carey Avenue, at West 24th Street. Seen from the street.',
  [['Laramie County Library: historic property research, an example','https://gowyld.libguides.com/HistoricPropertyResearch/Example'],['#1 Properties newsletter, March 2023','https://issuu.com/1properties/docs/02_march_2023_4-pg_newsletter_wt_-2']],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
]
